<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Irish Confederate Wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1641">1641</a>–<a href="/years/1653">1653</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Eleven Years' War · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-three-kingdoms">Wars of the Three Kingdoms</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Irish Confederate Wars were the Irish part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-three-kingdoms">Wars of the Three Kingdoms</a>, beginning with the Irish Rebellion of 1641. Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederate-ireland">Confederate Ireland</a>, governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilkenny">Kilkenny</a>, fought English and Scottish Protestant forces and the royalists in a complex war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>'s invasion of 1649 and the following conquest crushed the Catholic Irish, leading to large confiscations of land.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/irish-confederate-wars.jpg" alt="Irish Confederate Wars" width="320" height="283" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wenceslaus Hollar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wenceslaus_Hollar_%E2%80%93_supposed_Irish_atrocities_during_the_Rebellion_of_1641.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The rising of October 1641 was planned as a swift and mainly bloodless seizure of power by a small group of conspirators led by Phelim O'Neill, who meant to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin-castle">Dublin Castle</a> and win an end to anti-Catholic discrimination, more Irish self-government and a rollback of the Plantations of Ireland. The plot was betrayed at the last minute, and the rising turned into attacks on Protestant settlers in Ulster: around 4,000 Protestants were massacred and a further 12,000 may have died of privation. Settlers and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> government answered with massacres of Catholics, as on Rathlin Island.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>By the end of 1642 the Confederation based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilkenny">Kilkenny</a> controlled two-thirds of Ireland, and it later supported some 60,000 men under professionals such as Thomas Preston and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owen-roe-oneill">Owen Roe O'Neill</a>. The Adventurers' Act of March 1642 had funded the war with loans to be repaid from rebel land, committing the English to confiscation. A ceasefire with the Royalists on 15 September 1643 was followed by years of raids in which all sides destroyed crops. Owen Roe O'Neill defeated Robert Munro's army at Benburb on 5 June 1646, but defeats in 1647 and a treaty with the Royalists divided the Confederates.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a> landed in August 1649 with the New Model Army and secured the east coast, massacring the defenders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drogheda">Drogheda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wexford">Wexford</a>. Kilkenny fell in March 1650 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galway">Galway</a> in May 1652, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> known as Tories fought on until the last organised force surrendered in Cavan in April 1653. The English answered with evictions and the destruction of crops, and famine and bubonic plague followed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>William Petty, who made the Down Survey in the 1650s, put the dead at between 400,000 and 620,000 in a population of about 1.5 million; the lowest estimate is about 200,000, about two-thirds of them civilians. Ireland was annexed by the English Commonwealth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> was repressed, and tens of thousands were sent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> as indentured servants or joined Catholic armies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. After the Act for the Settlement of Ireland of 1652, the share of land owned by Protestants rose from 41% in 1641 to 78% in 1660, and Irish poetry long lamented the Confederates' disunity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Irish Confederate Wars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Irish Confederate Wars</b>, begun with the rising of 1641, caused an estimated 200,000 deaths.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-15">15 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
